cartridge "THE ADVENTURER/J-F HEON/1987"

' THE ADVENTURER
' A ColecoVision game based on "The Adventurer" by Jean-Francois Heon,
' Ahoy! February 1987 (Commodore 64, BASIC; the maze screens and the
' character set in the DJ file on the disk ahoy1987-02).
' Made for Amy Studio by Claude (an AI made by Anthropic), 2026, in Amy by
' Amy Bienvenu.
'
' Not a cell-for-cell port: the rules are the original's (read from its
' listing, orig/adventurer.bas), the game is built for the ColecoVision:
' smooth 16 x 16 sprites gliding from cell to cell, stone wall tiles with
' lit and shadowed edges, sound effects and an original title tune.
'
' The game: you are shut in a maze of twelve rooms (four across, three
' down). Twelve keys lie in the rooms, numbered 1 to 12, and they must be
' picked up in order: the HUD shows the number of the next key; a key with
' another number is a wall for now. Each room that still holds its key is
' guarded by a monster that walks straight at you; touch it and the game
' is over. Shoot it with the button (a bolt flies five cells in the way
' you face): it is gone until you come back into the room. With all twelve
' keys every room has its monster again; get back to the first room (top
' left) and the door opens.
'
' From the original: the twelve rooms (orig/dj.prg, the C64 screens at
' $4800-$77FF), the keys' places and numbers (DATA lines 185-195), the
' start (7, 7 in the top-left room), moving one cell at a time, leaving a
' room by any opening into the next room, the keys in order (a wrong key
' blocks with a sound), 100 points a key and 50 a monster, one monster in
' a room with its key, starting next to the key and gone once the key is
' taken; shot monsters return when the room is entered again; with all
' keys a monster in every room; the bolt of five cells stopped by walls
' and keys; the score and key number at the bottom; the end
' text (YOU ARE OPENING THE DOOR... YOU'RE ESCAPING, THE END, MAYBE...).
'
' Liberties: the 40 x 24 C64 screens are resampled to 28 x 21 cells (a
' cell is a wall only where the whole C64 area it covers is wall, so the
' diagonal passages stay open). The monster is slower than you (a cell in
' 12 frames, you 8): in the listing both moved one cell a pass, and with
' the monster's step on the same pass as yours a player could not get
' away. When its straight way is blocked, the monster tries the other
' axis instead of standing still (on the C64 it froze against any wall,
' which made every room safe once you stood behind a wall). The monster
' waits 45 frames when you enter its room (on the C64 it moved at once). A
' monster scores once a room until the next key (on the C64 leaving and
' coming back gave 50 points again and again). The bolt
' flies while you walk (the C64 stopped the game while it flew). Holding
' the stick while pressing the button shoots that way without a step (on
' the C64 you shot the way of your last step only). Touching
' the monster is checked on the sprites' positions (the C64 compared
' cells): the adventurer within 6 pixels; the stiletto by a box fitted to
' the drawings, up to 7 pixels across its flight (a pierced wing or horn
' kills, a gargoyle centred in the next row or column does not), along it
' from 5 lit pixels of overlap (BoltHit). A high score. Three lives instead of one: a
' caught adventurer starts again where he came into the room, with the
' room's monster back at its place.
'
' Story and names (from the Ahoy! article, told in our own words on three
' STORY pages from the title menu, with a how-to-play page that does not
' give the order or a map): the keys are shown as gold coffers and called
' COFFER on the status line (GET OUT once all are open), the monsters are
' gargoyles (grey and dark red, two frames, a change every 16 frames), the
' bolt is a stiletto pointing the way it flies. The title has a PLAY /
' STORY menu (up/down, a button); the high score moved from the status
' line to the title and the game-over box.
'
' Controls: either controller; the stick walks (hold to keep walking), a
' button shoots. On the credits, a button goes on; on the title, up and
' down pick PLAY or STORY, a button chooses; on the story pages a button
' or a push of the stick turns the page.
'
' House rules: text in columns 2-29, rows 1-22; tiles from code 128; the
' frame loop is protected from the NMI; screens drawn with the NMI off
' after a wait, display off. Music first in the sound table, effects after;
' the title tune is played once. `set sprite count` is always given.

